**Epilepsy** is a condition that affects the brain and causes **repeated seizures**. A seizure is a burst of electrical activity in the brain that temporarily affects how it works. Around **630,000 people** in the UK have epilepsy.

## Types of seizures

Seizures can look very different:

- **focal seizures** start in one part of the brain. You may feel strange, have odd sensations, or be unaware of what you are doing
- **tonic clonic seizures** (once called grand mal) where you lose consciousness, go stiff, fall and your body jerks
- **absence seizures**, where you "switch off" for a few seconds
- **atonic seizures**, where muscles go floppy and you may fall

## Causes

Often no cause is found. Epilepsy can be linked to a **brain injury**, **stroke**, **brain infection**, **brain tumour**, or genes.

## Getting diagnosed

You will see a specialist, usually within 2 weeks of a first seizure. It helps to bring a description or **video** of the seizure from someone who saw it. Tests may include an **EEG**, which records brain activity, and an **MRI** scan.

{% callout type="danger" title="First aid for a seizure" %}
- **stay calm**, note the time, and stay with them
- move dangerous objects away and **cushion their head**
- do **not** hold them down or put anything in their mouth
- when the jerking stops, put them in the **recovery position** and stay until they recover

Call **999** if the seizure lasts more than **5 minutes**, one seizure follows another, they are injured, it is their first seizure, or they do not recover.
{% /callout %}

Read about [epilepsy treatment](/conditions/brain-nerves-and-senses/epilepsy/treatment/).
